FINDINGS:
1. [MAJOR] tests/common/corpus.rs:363 — `compare` worth-mismatch message swaps actual and recorded: it prints `expected.value.tagged()` as "the program is worth" and `value.tagged()` as "records" — every value divergence misnames which side is which — swap the two format args so `value.tagged()` is "worth" and `expected.value.tagged()` is "records".
2. [MAJOR] tests/common/generator.rs:758 — `objects` family declares `object Undefaulted` but reads `say Tagged.tag` (corpus/objects-0003.rb:4 records `AnalyzerError: Unknown variable 'Tagged'`), so the "has without default" row is never exercised and the claimed coverage is an unknown-variable test already covered by `malformed` — change the tail to `say Undefaulted.tag`.
3. [MAJOR] tests/common/vm.rs:84 — `Typed::tagged` writes container elements with no escaping and `Typed::parse` reads them via `split_top`/ `split_once(": ")` (tests/common/vm.rs:121), so any text containing `,` or `:` inside a list/record breaks round-trip (e.g. `list:[text:a, b]` splits into two items); the corpus only avoids it because `WORDS` has no delimiters — escape delimiters in `tagged` or quote text payloads.
4. [MAJOR] tests/common/generator.rs:780 — `objects` tail uses `say upper(Named.name)` (corpus/objects-0019.rb:4 records `Unknown function 'upper'`; the builtin is `uppercase`) and tests/common/generator.rs:1028 puts `uppercase("a")` in `value-tails` (corpus/value-tails-0020.rb:1 records `Unknown function 'uppercase'`), so two families pin stdlib-resolution failures instead of object/value-tail behavior and overstate family coverage — use `length`/`type_of` or move these programs to `faults`.
REVIEW VERDICT: FINDINGS 4
